WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.760
A cell in a rabbit's eye fires hard for a spot moving right.

2
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.180
Moving left, it barely fires.

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.200
How does it know?

4
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.790
The direction that drives it is its preferred direction.

5
00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.710
The opposite way is its null direction.

6
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:23.113
Barlow and Hill reported such cells in rabbit retina in 1963.

7
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:27.383
Two years later, Barlow and Levick asked how it works.

8
00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:33.297
They flashed two neighbouring spots in the cell's field, spot A and spot B, one

9
00:00:33.297 --> 00:00:34.297
after the other.

10
00:00:35.017 --> 00:00:38.257
A then B is a small jump in the preferred direction.

11
00:00:39.237 --> 00:00:41.197
B then A is a jump in the null direction.

12
00:00:42.217 --> 00:00:46.737
If the two flashes simply added, the pair would reach the dashed line in either

13
00:00:46.737 --> 00:00:47.737
order.

14
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:52.167
Predict. Under a veto, which order falls below the line?

15
00:00:53.087 --> 00:00:55.207
Under a boost, which order rises above it?

16
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.600
A veto leaves a deficit in the null order.

17
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.840
A boost leaves a surplus in the preferred order.

18
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.050
Barlow and Levick found a big deficit in the null order.

19
00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:13.650
The preferred order gave about the sum.

20
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:17.033
So the main operation in this cell is a veto.

21
00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:24.967
They also inferred that the veto is local, working in small subunits across the

22
00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:25.967
field.

23
00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:27.617
Here is a simple sketch.

24
00:01:28.497 --> 00:01:31.977
To cancel excitation, inhibition must overlap it in time.

25
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:37.187
Null motion should start the inhibition early, so it should come from where null

26
00:01:37.187 --> 00:01:39.787
motion starts. Call that the null side.

27
00:01:40.817 --> 00:01:43.737
Preferred motion reaches the excitation first.

28
00:01:44.677 --> 00:01:48.197
The inhibition lags behind, so the excitation gets a head start.

29
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.760
Barlow and Levick were tentative about which cells supply it.

30
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.580
Most of it turned out to come from starburst amacrine cells.

31
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.200
Its dendrites radiate like the spokes of a wheel.

32
00:02:01.183 --> 00:02:03.983
Each dendrite is its own small direction detector.

33
00:02:04.923 --> 00:02:08.663
It is most active when motion runs outward, from cell body to tip.

34
00:02:09.633 --> 00:02:12.593
Take a ganglion cell that prefers rightward motion.

35
00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:17.853
It is wired mainly to starburst branches that point left, its null direction.

36
00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:22.053
Leftward motion runs outward along those branches.

37
00:02:22.993 --> 00:02:26.433
Their inhibition is strongest, and the ganglion cell stays quiet.

38
00:02:27.517 --> 00:02:30.577
Rightward motion runs inward along the same branches.

39
00:02:31.477 --> 00:02:33.637
They release little, so the excitation wins.

40
00:02:36.767 --> 00:02:38.047
So remember the sign.

41
00:02:38.887 --> 00:02:43.747
The strongest inhibition comes from dendrites that prefer roughly the direction the

42
00:02:43.747 --> 00:02:44.887
ganglion cell ignores.
